add('jpn_2023_414dca',4,'Bounding the product between consecutive squares determines a; a shifted factorization then reduces the rest to a controlled divisor-pair list.',r'''Write the quotient as $(a-1/b)(a-1/c)$. It lies in $[(a-1)^2,a^2)$, and $44^2<2023<45^2$, so $a=45$. The equation becomes
$$(2b-45)(2c-45)=2023=7\cdot17^2.$$
Both factors are odd and at least $-43$. They cannot both be negative, since then their product is at most $43^2<2023$. Ordered positive factor pairs are $(1,2023),(7,289),(17,119)$. They give $c=1034,167,82$, respectively, and each yields positive integers satisfying the equation. Thus $\boxed{c\in\{82,167,1034\}}$.''',statement=r'''Positive integers $a,b,c$ satisfy $b\le c$ and $(ab-1)(ac-1)=2023bc$. Determine every possible value of $c$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Diophantine equations','Divisibility & GCD','Squares & powers'])
add('jpn_2023_ccf086',5.5,'Parity bounds the number of unsuccessful edges via six odd faces; equality fixes the internal edges and leaves two parity patterns whose even entries are free.',r'''Call an edge bad when its labels have the same parity. The graph consists of a central pentagon and five surrounding pentagonal faces. Every pentagon contains an odd number of bad edges. If $a$ counts bad outer edges and $b$ bad internal edges, summing over the six faces gives $a+2b\ge6$, hence at least three bad edges.

Equality requires no bad outer edge and one bad edge in every face. The central pentagon has exactly one bad edge, which can be chosen in five ways. It also serves its adjacent outer face; the four remaining outer faces must be paired by two bad spokes. On the resulting four-face path this pairing is unique. Thus there are five possible bad-edge sets. For each, the connected graph fixes all label parities once one vertex parity is chosen. Following the outer cycle and spokes gives parity-class sizes $7$ and $8$. These assignments realize exactly three bad edges, so the maximal score is $17$. Since an odd label must be $1$ but an even label can be either $0$ or $2$, the number of maximizing labelings is $\boxed{5(2^7+2^8)=1920}$.''',statement=r'''Form a graph with outer vertices $v_0,\ldots,v_9$ in a cycle and inner vertices $u_0,\ldots,u_4$ in a cycle, adding spokes $u_i v_{2i}$ for $0\le i<5$. Label each vertex by $0,1$ or $2$. A good edge has endpoint labels differing by $1$. How many labelings maximize the number of good edges? All vertices are distinguished.''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Graph theory','Colouring','Parity','Double counting'],note='The original diagram is rendered as an exact graph description; parity classes and edge patterns independently verified.')
add('jpn_2023_b42b43',5,'The total variation is only two above its minimum, forcing a unique backwards step of size one; a subset choice then encodes every permutation.',r'''Append zeros at both ends and split the path at its entry $2023$. A monotone climb and descent has total variation $4046$. The extra variation is twice the sum of all downward steps before the peak and upward steps afterwards. Therefore there is exactly one step against the main direction, and its length is $1$.

Suppose it occurs after the peak, as $m,m+1$, where $1\le m\le2021$. Every other value below $2023$ may be assigned freely to the increasing left side or the decreasing right side. The assigned sets and $m$ determine the permutation uniquely: on the right, insert $m,m+1$ between the values larger and smaller than these two. There are $2^{2020}$ assignments. Reversing the permutation accounts for the equally numerous case before the peak. Hence the count is $\boxed{2021\cdot2^{2021}}$.''',statement=r'''Count permutations $(p_1,\ldots,p_{2023})$ of $1,\ldots,2023$ satisfying $p_1+\sum_{i=1}^{2022}|p_{i+1}-p_i|+p_{2023}=4048$.''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Permutations & arrangements','Bijections','Counting'])
add('jpn_2023_12ba06',5,'A tangent-style similarity produces a power product, and the midpoint of a right-triangle hypotenuse transfers it to a second similarity before angle chasing.',r'''The triangles $MBA$ and $MAP$ are similar, so $MB\cdot MP=MA^2$. Since $\angle ADC=90^\circ$ and $M$ is the midpoint of $AC$, we have $MA=MD=MC$. Thus $MBD\sim MDP$, giving $\angle DBM=\angle MDP$. Combining this with the isosceles triangle $MCD$ yields
$$\angle AMP=\angle CBM+\angle MCB=\angle MDP+\angle CDM=\angle CDP=65^\circ.$$
Set $\theta=\angle PAM=\angle MBA$. In triangle $ABM$, $41^\circ+\theta+\theta+65^\circ=180^\circ$, so $\theta=37^\circ$. Therefore $\boxed{\angle BAC=78^\circ}$.''',statement=r'''In acute triangle $ABC$, let $D$ be the altitude foot from $A$ and $M$ the midpoint of $AC$. A point $P$ on $BM$ satisfies $\angle PAM=\angle MBA$. If $\angle BAP=41^\circ$ and $\angle PDB=115^\circ$, find $\angle BAC$.''',topic='Geometry',subtopics=['Similarity & congruence','Angles & cyclic quadrilaterals','Triangle centres'])
add('jpn_2023_b7080f',6.5,'The winning states are sets closed under prime support; recognizing that closure gives a response strategy and also proves every other first move loses.',r'''For a nonempty set $Q$ of primes at most $50$, let $X(Q)$ contain the numbers in $\{2,\ldots,50\}$ whose prime factors all belong to $Q$. We show that a player who leaves the total erased set equal to $X(Q)$ can force a win.

Suppose the opponent next erases $T$. Every element of $T$ has a prime factor outside $Q$. Let $R$ be all such new primes. The opponent cannot have erased the primes in $R$ themselves: they are coprime to every number in the preceding move, whose factors lie in $Q$. The response $X(Q\cup R)\setminus(X(Q)\cup T)$ is therefore nonempty. Every number in that response contains a prime of $R$, shared with some number of $T$, so the response is legal and restores the claimed form. Since numbers are continually erased, this strategy wins.

If Alice's first set $S$ has prime support $Q$ but is not $X(Q)$, Bob can erase the nonempty complement $X(Q)\setminus S$, legally, and take over this strategy. Thus exactly the sets $X(Q)$ are winning first moves. There are fifteen primes at most $50$, and distinct prime sets give distinct closures. The answer is $\boxed{2^{15}-1}$.''',statement=r'''Initially the integers $2,3,\ldots,50$ are on a board. Alice first erases a chosen nonempty set $S$. Thereafter Bob and Alice alternate, erasing a nonempty set of remaining numbers, each of which must share a prime factor with some number erased on the preceding turn. A player with no legal move loses. For how many first sets $S$ can Alice force a win?''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Games & strategies','Sets & partitions','Invariants'])
add('jpn_2023_648f4e',7,'A common fixed point and a common constant interval must be extracted from the composition law; shifting monotone functions turns the family into a binomial subset bound.',r'''Write $N=2023$. A nonincreasing function has at most one fixed point. Taking $g=f$ in the composition law shows that $f(f(x))$ is fixed, so a fixed point exists. If $a$ is fixed by $f$, the law with the two functions interchanged gives $g(a)=f(g(a))$, forcing $g(a)=a$. Thus every function has the same fixed point $a$, and the original law now says $f(g(x))=a$.

Let $I$ be the set of inputs on which every function equals $a$. It is an integer interval $[\ell,m]$ by monotonicity, and every function has its image in this interval. For each function define $s_x=f(x)-x$. These are strictly decreasing integers between $\ell-N$ and $m-1$, containing the fixed block $a-m,\ldots,a-\ell$. Outside that block there are exactly $N-1$ available integers, of which $N-m+\ell-1$ must be selected. Different functions give different selections, so the family has size at most
$$\binom{N-1}{N-m+\ell-1}\le\binom{2022}{1011}.$$
For equality take all nonincreasing functions with $f(1)=\cdots=f(1012)=1012$ and arbitrary nonincreasing values in $[1,1012]$ thereafter. Every composition is constantly $1012$, and the number of these functions is the central binomial coefficient above. Thus the maximum is $\boxed{\binom{2022}{1011}}$.''',statement=r'''Let $A$ be a family of functions from $\{1,\ldots,2023\}$ to itself. Every member is nonincreasing, and $f(g(x))=g(f(g(x)))$ for all $f,g\in A$ and all inputs $x$. Determine the largest possible size of $A$.''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Sets & partitions','Bijections','Counting'])
