add('jpn_2023_cc8de7',4.5,'Two sparse coordinate parity classes occur once in every skew tetromino; the multiplicity cap bounds the number of tiles, and an explicit overlapping construction attains it.',r'''Index rows and columns by $1,\ldots,5$. Mark the four cells with both coordinates even as class $A$, and the nine cells with both coordinates odd as class $B$. Every allowed skew tetromino covers exactly one cell of each class, in any orientation. Since an $A$ cell can be covered at most twice, there are at most eight tiles. Hence at least one of the nine $B$ cells remains uncovered, giving an upper bound of $24$.

The bound is attained by the following eight tiles, each specified by its four $(\text{column},\text{row})$ cells:
$$\begin{aligned}
&\{(1,5),(2,4),(2,5),(3,4)\},\quad\{(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1)\},\\
&\{(1,3),(1,4),(2,2),(2,3)\},\quad\{(4,3),(4,4),(5,2),(5,3)\},\\
&\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,2),(2,3)\},\quad\{(4,3),(4,4),(5,4),(5,5)\},\\
&\{(2,1),(3,1),(3,2),(4,2)\},\quad\{(2,4),(3,4),(3,5),(4,5)\}.
\end{aligned}$$
They cover every cell except $(3,3)$, and none more than twice. Thus the maximum is $\boxed{24}$.''',statement=r'''Place copies of the skew tetromino with cells $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(2,1)$ on a $5\times5$ grid. Rotations and reflections are allowed, and tiles may overlap, but no cell may be covered more than twice and no tile may leave the board. What is the greatest number of covered cells?''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Colouring','Double counting','Tilings & dissections'],note='Replaced the original diagram by exact cell coordinates and supplied an independently checked attaining construction.')
add('jpn_2023_9e5c0d',4.5,'The parallel line creates a second cyclic quadrilateral; two midpoint parallels then identify an orthocentre.',r'''Because $AX\perp DX$ and $AY\perp DY$, the points $A,D,X,Y$ are cyclic. Since $FP\parallel XY$ and $D,P,Y$ are collinear, their angle relations imply that $A,D,F,P$ are cyclic. Now $DF\parallel AC$ by the midpoint theorem, so $DF\perp DP$. Cyclicity therefore gives $AF\perp AP$, hence $AB\perp AP$. But $DE\parallel AB$, so $DE\perp AP$, while $AE\perp DP$ by construction. Thus $E$ is the orthocentre of triangle $ADP$, and its third altitude is $EP\perp AD$.''',statement=r'''In acute triangle $ABC$, let $D,E,F$ be the midpoints of $BC,CA,AB$. Drop perpendiculars from $D$ to $AB,AC$, meeting them at $X,Y$. The line through $F$ parallel to $XY$ meets $DY$ at $P\ne E$. Prove $EP\perp AD$.''',topic='Geometry',subtopics=['Angles & cyclic quadrilaterals','Triangle centres'])
add('jpn_2023_4980a0',6.5,'Comparing counting thresholds rules out decreases; an eventually increasing sequence has bounded monotone offsets, which stabilize and then determine the entire sequence backwards.',r'''Let $F(t)$ count the indices $i$ with $a_i\le t$ whenever that count is finite. The condition is $a_n=F(a_{n+1}+c)$. If $a_n>a_{n+1}$ then monotonicity of $F$ forces $a_{n+1}>a_{n+2}$, producing an impossible infinite decreasing positive sequence. Thus the sequence is nondecreasing. Likewise a strict increase propagates forwards. It cannot be constant forever, since then one of the prescribed counts would be infinite. It is therefore strictly increasing from some index $k$ onwards.

For $n\ge k$, strict integral growth gives $a_i>a_{n+1}+c$ whenever $i>n+c+1$. Hence $a_n\le n+c+1$. The integers $a_n-n$ are nondecreasing for $n\ge k$ and bounded above by $c+1$, so eventually $a_n=n+d$. In that tail the count at threshold $a_{n+1}+c$ is exactly $n+c+1$, showing $d=c+1$.

If the formula holds from $N\ge2$ onwards, monotonicity shows that $a_i\le a_N+c$ holds exactly for $i\le N+c$. The defining condition gives $a_{N-1}=N+c$, extending the formula one step backwards. Thus the unique sequence is $\boxed{a_n=n+c+1}$, which directly satisfies the condition.''',statement=r'''Fix a nonnegative integer $c$. Determine every sequence of positive integers $(a_n)_{n\ge1}$ for which, for each $n\ge1$, exactly $a_n$ indices $i\ge1$ satisfy $a_i\le a_{n+1}+c$.''',topic='Algebra',subtopics=['Sequences & recurrences','Functions & graphs'])
add('jpn_2023_3afbc7',7,'Coprimality first forces squarefreeness; multiplicative orders give strong 2-adic lower bounds, leaving two prime-count cases that must be eliminated separately.',r'''The answer is $n=2$, which works directly; $n=1$ does not. If $n$ is even, divisibility of $\phi(n)^{d(n)}+1$ forces $\phi(n)$ odd, hence $n=2$.

Suppose $n\ge3$ is odd. The first condition forces $\gcd(n,\phi(n))=1$, so $n$ is squarefree. Write $n=p_1\cdots p_k$; then $d(n)=2^k$. For each prime $p_i$, the congruence $\phi(n)^{2^k}\equiv-1\pmod{p_i}$ shows that the order of $\phi(n)$ modulo $p_i$ is exactly $2^{k+1}$. Thus every $p_i\equiv1\pmod{2^{k+1}}$, and
$$v_2(\phi(n))\ge k(k+1),\qquad v_2(n-1)\ge k+1.$$
Successive factorization of $x^{2t}-1$ gives $v_2(x^y-1)\ge v_2(x-1)+v_2(y)$ for odd $x$. The second condition therefore yields $5k>(k+1)^2$, forcing $k=1$ or $2$.

For $k=1$, the first quotient is $n-2+2/n$, not an integer. For $k=2$, the bounds and $v_2(n^{\phi(n)}-1)<10$ force $v_2(n-1)=3$ and $v_2(p_i-1)=3$ for both primes. Hence both primes are $9$ modulo $16$, so $n\equiv1\pmod{16}$, a contradiction. No odd $n$ works.''',statement=r'''Let $\phi(n)$ be Euler's totient and $d(n)$ the number of positive divisors of $n$. Find all positive integers $n$ for which $n\mid\phi(n)^{d(n)}+1$, but $d(n)^5$ does not divide $n^{\phi(n)}-1$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Arithmetic functions','Multiplicative order','Valuations','Primes'])
