add('jpn_2022_a7e14f',4,'Eliminating the freely varying k produces a fixed integer divisible by arbitrarily large d; its vanishing is then both necessary and constructively sufficient.',r'''If $d\mid m-k^2$ and $d\mid n+2k$, then
$$d\mid4(m-k^2)-(n-2k)(n+2k)=4m-n^2.$$
Arbitrarily large positive divisors are possible only when $4m=n^2$. Thus $(m,n)=(t^2,2t)$ for a positive integer $t$. Conversely, for any $N$, choosing $k=N$ and $d=t+N$ works because $(m-k^2)/d=t-N$ and $(n+2k)/d=2$. The bounds reduce to $1\le t\le44$, so there are $\boxed{44}$ pairs.''',statement=r'''Count integer pairs $1\le m,n\le2022$ with this property: for every positive integer $N$, some integers $k\ge0$ and $d>N$ satisfy $d\mid m-k^2$ and $d\mid n+2k$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Divisibility & GCD','Diophantine equations'])
add('jpn_2022_183337',4,'An auxiliary perpendicular creates two similar 45-degree triangles, converting the given two lengths into a ratio on the hypotenuse.',r'''Let $P$ be the intersection of the line $AB$ with the line through $D$ perpendicular to $BC$. The right angles at $D$ give $\angle CDE=\angle PDF$, and both $\angle DCE$ and $\angle DPF$ are $45^\circ$. Hence $PDF\sim CDE$ with ratio $DF/DE=3/5$. The right isosceles triangle $BPD$ gives $BD=DP$, so $BD:DC=3:5$. Since $BC=7\sqrt2$, the answer is $\boxed{21\sqrt2/8}$.''',statement=r'''In a right isosceles triangle $ABC$, $\angle A=90^\circ$ and $AB=AC=7$. Points $D\in BC$, $E\in CA$ and $F\in AB$ satisfy $DE\perp DF$, $DE=5$ and $DF=3$. Find $BD$.''',topic='Geometry',subtopics=['Similarity & congruence','Triangle geometry'])
add('jpn_2022_135c78',4,'Bounding square differences limits every gap to one or two and confines a two-step gap to the first three positions, giving a short exhaustive classification.',r'''The inequalities say $a_{i+1}^2-a_i^2\le2i+11$, and strict increase gives $a_i\ge i$. A gap at least three would give $6i+9>2i+11$, impossible. If all gaps equal one, write $a_i=i+c$; precisely $c=0,\ldots,5$ work, giving six sequences.

If a gap of two occurs at index $j$, then $4a_j+4\le2j+11$, whence $j\le3$. If $j$ is the last such index, then for $j=1$ the possibilities are $a_1=1,2$; for $j=2$ necessarily $(a_1,a_2)=(1,2)$; for $j=3$ necessarily $(a_1,a_2,a_3)=(1,2,3)$. In each case the remaining gaps are one and the inequalities hold. These give four more sequences. The total is $\boxed{10}$.''',statement=r'''Count strictly increasing positive integer sequences $a_1<\cdots<a_{2022}$ such that $a_1^2-6^2\ge a_2^2-7^2\ge\cdots\ge a_{2022}^2-2027^2$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Sequences & recurrences','Squares & powers'])
add('jpn_2022_e8d416',5.5,'Every nontrivial permutation cycle must pass through the exceptional index; it becomes a divisibility chain, whose count obeys a divisor recurrence.',r'''View the permutation as a bijection $f$. Every cycle avoiding $1000$ has $f(i)\ge i$ at each step, so it must be a fixed point. The cycle containing $1000$ is a chain $d_1\mid d_2\mid\cdots\mid d_\ell=1000$ with strictly increasing entries, followed by the return to $d_1$. Conversely each such chain defines one permitted permutation.

Let $c(n)$ count increasing divisor chains ending at $n$. Then $c(1)=1$ and $c(n)=1+\sum_{d\mid n,\,d<n}c(d)$. For the divisors $2^a5^b$ of $1000$, the table with rows $b=0,1,2,3$ and columns $a=0,1,2,3$ is
$$\begin{pmatrix}1&2&4&8\\2&6&16&40\\4&16&52&152\\8&40&152&504\end{pmatrix}.$$
Therefore the answer is $\boxed{504}$.''',statement=r'''How many permutations $(p_1,\ldots,p_{1000})$ of $1,\ldots,1000$ satisfy $i\mid p_i$ for every $1\le i\le999$?''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Permutations & arrangements','Bijections','Recurrences & generating functions'])
add('jpn_2022_60aa31',6,'Odd-part chains force one selection per chain; several cross-chain divisibilities must be resolved before the remaining independent choices can be multiplied.',r'''Partition $1,\ldots,50$ into chains $\{q,2q,4q,\ldots\}\cap[1,50]$ indexed by odd $q$. A valid 25-element set chooses one element of each of the 25 chains. Every odd number from $27$ through $49$ is forced. They in turn force the choices $26$ and $30$ in the $13$ and $15$ chains, and restrict the $9,3,1,5$ chains to $\{18,36\},\{12,24,48\},\{8,16,32\},\{20,40\}$.

The chains indexed by $11,17,19,23,25$ each give two independent choices. The $7,21$ chains give exactly $(14,21),(28,21),(28,42)$, independently of the others. In the four remaining chains indexed by $1,3,5,9$, choosing $8$ permits only $(12,20,18)$; choosing $16$ gives two choices for the $5$ chain and the three pairs $(12,18),(24,18),(24,36)$ for the $3,9$ chains; choosing $32$ gives the same two choices and those three pairs plus $(48,18),(48,36)$. These exhaust cross-chain divisibilities. Their total is $1+2\cdot3+2\cdot5=17$. Thus the answer is $\boxed{2^5\cdot3\cdot17=1632}$.''',statement=r'''Count the 25-element subsets of $\{1,2,\ldots,50\}$ in which no chosen number divides another chosen number.''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Sets & partitions','Counting','Pigeonhole principle'])
add('jpn_2022_b3adee',6,'Expanding by decimal digits makes every multinomial term cancel unless each digit occurs to positive degree; the unique survivor gives the exact 5-adic valuation.',r'''Include the zero term and write each number below $10^{100}$ as $n=\sum_{i=0}^{99}a_i10^i$, where $0\le a_i\le9$. Expand $n^{100}$ by the multinomial theorem and sum with sign $(-1)^{\sum a_i}$. Any term omitting one digit variable cancels, since $\sum_{a=0}^9(-1)^a=0$. The only term involving all 100 variables has every exponent equal to one. Therefore
$$S=100!\prod_{i=0}^{99}10^i\prod_{i=0}^{99}\left(\sum_{a=0}^9(-1)^a a\right)=100!\,10^{4950}(-5)^{100}.$$
Legendre's formula gives $v_5(100!)=20+4=24$. Hence $\boxed{v_5(S)=4950+100+24=5074}$.''',statement=r'''For positive $n$, let $f(n)=(-1)^{s(n)}n^{100}$, where $s(n)$ is its decimal digit sum. Determine the highest power of $5$ dividing $\sum_{n=1}^{10^{100}-1}f(n)$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Digits & bases','Valuations','Factorials & binomial coefficients'],note='Corrected the source cancellation condition: the cancelled terms are those with at least one zero digit exponent.')
add('jpn_2022_9e8b52',5,'An auxiliary second circle intersection turns the angle condition into equality of chords in equal-radius circles; midpoint power then determines BC.',r'''The circumcircles of $ABC$ and $BHC$ have equal radii, since $\angle BHC=180^\circ-\angle BAC$. Let $Q\ne P$ be the second intersection of $PM$ with the circle $BHC$. Using its cyclic angles and the given condition gives $\angle PBQ=\angle PBC+\angle CPQ=\angle ABC$. The sine rule in the two equal-radius circles yields $PQ=AC=10$. Since $M$ lies inside the circle and $PM=3$, we have $MQ=7$. Power at $M$ gives $MB\cdot MC=MP\cdot MQ=21$. As $MB=MC$, the answer is $\boxed{BC=2\sqrt{21}}$.''',statement=r'''In acute triangle $ABC$, let $AB=11$, $AC=10$, $H$ be the orthocentre and $M$ the midpoint of $BC$. A point $P$ inside the triangle lies on the circumcircle of $BHC$, with $\angle ABP=\angle CPM$ and $PM=3$. Determine $BC$.''',topic='Geometry',subtopics=['Triangle centres','Power of a point & radical axes','Angles & cyclic quadrilaterals'])
add('jpn_2022_269940',5,'Counting changes fixes the game length; growing the protected endpoint block gives matching strategies for both players.',r'''A move removes exactly two boundaries between different names. Initially there are $2021$ boundaries. The leftmost name always belongs to Alex and the rightmost to Betty, so the boundary count is odd; with at least three boundaries either player has an interior opponent block to take. Thus play ends with one boundary after $1010$ moves, $505$ per player.

Alex always captures the first Betty block immediately to the right of his left endpoint block. His protected block then grows by at least two cells and cannot subsequently be recaptured, so he guarantees $1+2\cdot505=1011$ cells. Betty uses the symmetric strategy at the right endpoint and guarantees the same number. Therefore Alex cannot force more, and the answer is $\boxed{1011}$.''',statement=r'''A row of 2022 cells alternates Alex's and Betty's names, starting with Alex on the left. Alex moves first. On a turn, a player selects two cells bearing their own name with a nonempty block of only the opponent's names between them, and changes that block to their own name. Play stops when the player to move has no legal move. What is the most cells Alex can guarantee bear his name at the end?''',topic='Combinatorics',subtopics=['Games & strategies','Invariants'])
add('jpn_2022_e889ae',5.5,'Diagonal substitution makes every orbit strictly increasing; two reordered substitutions then identify iterate exponents and collapse the functional equation.',r'''Putting $m=n$ gives $f(n)^2=n^2+f^{f(n)}(n)>n^2$, so $f(n)>n$ and every iterated orbit is strictly increasing. Comparing the substitutions $(m,n)=(f(t),t)$ and $(t,f(t))$ yields
$$f^{f(t)+1}(t)=f^{f(f(t))}(t).$$
Strict growth of the orbit forces $f(f(t))=f(t)+1$. Iterating this identity gives $f^k(t)=f(t)+k-1$ for $k\ge1$. Return to the diagonal equation to obtain $f(n)^2=n^2+2f(n)-1$, or $(f(n)-1)^2=n^2$. Positivity yields $f(n)=n+1$, and direct substitution verifies it. Thus $\boxed{f(n)=n+1}$ is the unique solution.''',statement=r'''Find all functions $f:\mathbb Z_{>0}\to\mathbb Z_{>0}$ such that $f^{f(n)}(m)+mn=f(m)f(n)$ for every positive $m,n$, where $f^k$ denotes $k$ iterations.''',topic='Algebra',subtopics=['Functional equations','Sequences & recurrences'])
add('jpn_2022_3901aa',None,'The proposed proof identifies an isosceles candidate centre by a doubled directed angle, but modulo-180 angle doubling alone does not settle the centre branch.',omitReason='Held unrated pending independent verification of the circumcentre identification and the possible tangent/intersection cases; the source explicitly flags a sidedness requirement.')
add('jpn_2022_8b0446',7,'Even exponents reduce to a difference-of-squares growth bound; odd exponents require a 3-adic lifting lemma before a cubic factorization gives a contradiction.',r'''If $y$ is even, reduction modulo $4$ forces $x$ even. Write $x=2z$, $y=2w$. Then
$$8zw+1=(3^z-8^w)(3^z+8^w)\ge3^z+8^w.$$
The positive first factor gives $z>w$, whence $3^z<8z^2+1$. Since $3^z>8z^2+1$ for $z\ge5$ (check $z=5$ and induct), $z\le4$. Thus $8zw+1\le65$, so $w=1$; checking $z=1,2,3,4$ gives only $z=2$, or $(x,y)=(4,2)$.

If $y$ is odd, factorization of $8^{3t}+1$ or the elementary lifting lemma gives $v_3(8^y+1)=v_3(y)+2$. Because $0<8^y+1<3^x$, its valuation is below $x$. The equation $8^y+1=3^x-2xy$ therefore gives $v_3(x)=2$. Write $x=3v$, with $v\ge3$. Now
$$6vy+1=3^{3v}-2^{3y}=(3^v-2^y)(9^v+3^v2^y+4^y)>9^v.$$
Positivity implies $y<2v$, hence $9^v<12v^2+1$. But the binomial theorem gives $9^v\ge1+8v+64\binom v2>12v^2+1$ for $v\ge3$, a contradiction. Thus the sole solution is $\boxed{(4,2)}$.''',statement=r'''Solve $3^x-8^y=2xy+1$ in positive integers $x,y$.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Diophantine equations','Valuations','Squares & powers'],note='Corrected the source odd-case factorization: the powers of 2 are indexed by y, not by x/3.')
(P/'reviews.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2));print('reviews',len(r))
